{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1454725-banality-disbelief","id":1454725,"title":"Banality & Disbelief","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Changeling: The Dreaming"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-07-12T00:17:33-07:00","id":1454725,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> Banality & Disbelief","content":"Hello community!<br /> <br /> I remember back in 2nd Edition that Banality and mortal witnesses/disbelief could erode fairy magic. I think I remember a specific example being that a changeling was flying using Wayfare could be in danger if mortals witnessed them because their Banality could erode the cantrip and cause the changeling to plummet to the ground. I've been trying to scour C20s new systems and am I right in saying that that risk/danger is gone from Rules As Written?<br /> <br /> I am trying to make sure that there are a lot of opportunities for my players to engage with Banality in meaningful ways in the narrative and not just as dots on a sheet. The very personal nature of Banality in C20 does a good job of this, and Banality Triggers being a roll instead of a carte blanch &quot;gain a dot of Banality&quot; means I feel more comfortable bringing it to the table more often because sometimes the roll succeeds, sometimes it fails. But in the past using C20 I have found that the game could use more systems for players to interface with Banality. Sometimes, using Rules As Written, Banality as a system is completely absent for many chapters and I'd like it to remain something that the players see out of the corner of their eye (if not a direct threat as appropriate, Autumn Person or Wall Street Finance Corporation).<br /> <br /> In addition to making sure I'm not missing anything in Rules As Written, I've made some house adjustments for my chronicle. For example, I'm not really a fan of how the Legacy Ban drains a point of Willpower from a changeling. I do not recall any other gameline having a similar penalty for Nature/Demeanor (could be wrong, I haven't played them all!). So, instead I have decided that the Primary Legacy Ban is an additional Banality Trigger because in acting on their Ban, the changeling is disassociating from the core of her faerie soul's story/dream. I like this because, again, a Banality Trigger is not an automatic point but a roll. It feels more fair than automatically deducting a point of Willpower and makes acting a Ban a more meaningful choice in the story (acting a Ban is a choice, after all). As suggested in Rules As Written, a Ban may be mitigated if the changeling is enacting their Secondary Legacy. It also feels satisfying that characters begin with a personal antithesis Trigger, a seeming Trigger, and a legacy Trigger. The rule of three, after all. <br /> <br /> Are there any other house rules people have found augment Banality's role as an interesting and meaningful presence in their chronicle?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13420-gl1tterboots","name":"gl1tterboots","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13420_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"58"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-12T08:31:23-07:00","id":1454744,"title":"","content":"So I\u2019m trying to recall and this might be a mix of RAW and what I do in my games but hopefully it will be helpful for you:<br /> <br /> So generally in C20 if a cantrip is Wyrd then it will effect waking reality and can do so even if perceived with The Mists stepping in to shield the Changeling and obscure the observer\u2019s perception. However if an ST prefers they could instead have it be that a waking observer obliviates the chimerical power at play, although that does place greater difficulties and limits on the changeling\u2019s power to cantrip.<br /> <br /> Something I do to have banality be more relevant, like with the personal banality triggers of each Changeling, is that I try to keep in mind the ambient banality of various characters and settings, which a Changeling must tangle with if they are exposed to or made to interact with them.<br /> <br /> I think that Ban house rule is pretty reasonable and makes sense imo. <br /> <br /> Plus as to your thread title and final question, I do think something like a \u2018Tinkerbell Rule\u2019 is fitting: in that, if a Changeling is exposed to a powerful wave of disbelief (whether that\u2019s a rejection of the Fae &amp; Magick or a rejection of theirs or another persons dreams and hopes, they are exposed to waves of banality.<br /> <br /> If I think of any other unique house rules I\u2019ll add them but this is what I\u2019ve got as of now.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-12T14:34:31-07:00","id":1454770,"title":"","content":"Thank you for the reply! First and foremost I wanted to make sure that I wasn't missing something Rules As Written before inventing something, and since that book is a CHONKER (which I love and adore) I thought I'd reach out and ask the community if I was missing something. <br /> <br /> I'm going back and forth in my head about it, because on the one hand that was the system I remember. But on the other hand, if something has been made Wyrd, it's generally a significant investment on the faerie's part in order to anchor that bit of chimerical reality into the Autumn World. In that respect, they've already &quot;paid the price&quot; to give it a measure of permanence, so that deserves to be respected. I'll give it up to the Mists at that point. <br /> <br /> It's worth exploring in the context of the narrative what happens when a faerie does something so bold in front of an unenchanted mortal to make them say &quot;this is impossible&quot; or &quot;this can't be happening&quot; or &quot;I don't believe this&quot; or any other such powerful rejections of the chimerical reality that the faerie is trying to manifest. What happens when the mortal does that? Is it something akin to Invoking the Autumn? Does the banality of that willful, stubborn disbelief clash with the faerie's willful, stubborn insistence that her chimerical reality IS real? It's an interesting question and I'm not sure how to handle that &quot;Tinkerbell Rule&quot; in the systems as written. I love that idea, though, and I think it deserves figuring out what happens in that scenario. What would that system look like?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13420-gl1tterboots","name":"gl1tterboots","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13420_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"58"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-23T18:39:45-07:00","id":1457533,"title":"","content":"From my perspective the sting of Banality is simplified more than removed in the game, although it have been toned down a little in some places.<br /> <br /> But generally speaking, as far as Cantrips are concerned, this was done by more strictly enforcing the casting as Wyrd. There is simply no way around it outside from doing the cantrip in the Dreaming. Virtually every cantrip that can generate this kind of reaction will be Wyrd anyways, period, always. I would just go further and end the effect if the character tries to circumvent the cost by using a Freehold before going on the streets, but even then only if it really shows to be disruptive.<br /> <br /> Instead of worrying about the cantrips, work on interpreting and expanding the Banality Triggers. It is a far simpler way to tune your game without creating unnecessary rules. You can decide on the fly that an action is a Banality Trigger if it makes sense for whatever reason, and have been working pretty fine on my games.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}